WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:17.710
Louis Pasteur was the chemist who has gone
down in history as one of the fathers of microbiology.

00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:22.020
He is famous for several accomplishments that
changed science as we know it, including the

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:26.880
pasteurization process of killing bacteria
from liquids, and paving the way for immunology

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:29.540
by creating life-saving vaccines.

00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:34.250
These accomplishments are just the tip of
the iceberg, because without Pasteur’s revolutionary

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:38.840
germ theory, modern medicine would not be
anything like it is today.

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:43.680
On this episode of Biographics, we are talking
about the life and work of Louis Pasteur,

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:49.000
and why he was one of the greatest minds in
history.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:56.000
Early Life and Education
Louis Pasteur was born in France on December

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:57.000
27, 1822.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:02.960
He was raised in a town called Arbois by his
parents, Jean-Joseph Pasteur, and Jeanne Etiennette

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:03.960
Roqui.

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:07.390
His father served as a sergeant major during
the Napoleonic Wars, and received the Legion

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:08.390
of Honor.

00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:12.530
After the war, Jean-Joseph worked as a full-time
leather tanner, like generations of Pasteur

00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:13.640
men before him.

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:17.971
Even though this was the family business,
Jean-Joseph did not want to force his own

00:01:17.971 --> 00:01:23.110
son to carry on the tanning trade, and was
open to allowing him to find his own passion.

00:01:23.110 --> 00:01:27.880
Usually, when we hear stories of accomplished
academic minds like Louis Pasteur, we imagine

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:30.750
that they may have been a genius all their
lives.

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:34.549
In reality, young Louis was a very average
student in school.

00:01:34.549 --> 00:01:39.060
As a young man, he was actually far more interested
in sketching works of art than reading his

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:40.060
textbooks.

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:43.950
He spent a lot of his time drawing and painting,
using the Jura Mountains as his inspiration.

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:47.780
Louis Pasteur’s father encouraged him to
follow his passion for art, and considered

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:51.250
the endeavour to be just as valuable as academic
goals.

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:55.220
Jean-Joseph always encouraged Louis to read
as many books as possible so that he remained

00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:57.070
curious about the world around him.

00:01:57.070 --> 00:02:02.600
As a veteran, he also instilled a sense of
national pride in Louis, and duty to the people

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:03.600
of France.

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:08.310
Since he spent most of his young life daydreaming
and sketching, Louis actually fell so far

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:13.239
behind academically, that he had to receive
a lot of tutoring at Collège d'Arbois, which

00:02:13.239 --> 00:02:17.739
is where he went to secondary school before
he attended university at the École Normale

00:02:17.739 --> 00:02:19.120
Supérieure in Paris.

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:21.750
This is where he began taking classes in science.

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:25.060
Chemistry became his new passion, and he had
the goal of earning his PhD.

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:29.800
In 1847, he received his doctorate degree,
and this set the course for the rest of his

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:31.060
life.

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:38.460
A Career in Chemistry
While he was earning his PhD, Louis Pasteur

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:43.310
taught chemistry at a secondary school called
Dijon Lycée before he became a research chemist

00:02:43.310 --> 00:02:44.530
at the University of Strasbourg.

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:49.099
When he was just 25 years old, he already
made discoveries that became vital in developing

00:02:49.099 --> 00:02:50.690
vaccines in the future.

00:02:50.690 --> 00:02:55.140
Microscopes had existed for hundreds of years
before Louis Pasteur was born, but new improvements

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:58.840
on the machine helped scientists to discover
the existence of microorganisms.

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:03.380
Louis Pasteur was far from the only scientist
to be studying microorganisms at the time,

00:03:03.380 --> 00:03:05.150
but he was one of the very first.

00:03:05.150 --> 00:03:10.220
He is widely considered to be one of the “fathers”
of the field that would later be called “microbiology.”

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:14.690
In 1849, Louis Pasteur was attempting to solve
issues that wine makers were having while

00:03:14.690 --> 00:03:15.910
fermenting grapes.

00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:20.770
Some batches of wine would go sour very quickly
while others were not, even when the ingredients

00:03:20.770 --> 00:03:23.830
and process were exactly the same every time.

00:03:23.830 --> 00:03:26.270
Wine makers could not figure out why this
was happening.

00:03:26.270 --> 00:03:30.099
Pasteur was able to gather tartaric acid,
which is one of the chemicals present in the

00:03:30.099 --> 00:03:33.760
sediment of the wine during the fermentation
process.

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:37.810
After dissolving the tartaric acid, Pasteur
was able to pass polarized light through the

00:03:37.810 --> 00:03:39.489
sample in order to observe it.

00:03:39.489 --> 00:03:44.440
He discovered that there was a nearly-identical
compound known as “paratartaric acid”

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:45.860
in the wine sediments.

00:03:45.860 --> 00:03:49.240
Every other scientist who had observed the
same samples assumed that the chemical was

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:53.060
the same, because it has the same composition
as tartaric acid.

00:03:53.060 --> 00:03:57.190
However, while these molecules seem identical
at first, they are actually mirror images

00:03:57.190 --> 00:03:58.190
of one another.

00:03:58.190 --> 00:04:02.170
You can think of it as being either left or
right-handed versions of the same chemical,

00:04:02.170 --> 00:04:04.580
with one of them causing the grapes to go
sour.

00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:08.790
Today, this process that Pasteur discovered
of finding the mirror version of a chemical

00:04:08.790 --> 00:04:13.860
compound is known as “molecular chirality”,
and it is actually extremely important in

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:18.430
pharmaceutical chemistry, because a left-or-right
handed version may react completely differently

00:04:18.430 --> 00:04:23.330
once it is metabolised in the human body,
even though it has the same chemical composition.

00:04:23.330 --> 00:04:27.110
His discovery in the variations between the
structure and shape of a molecule also led

00:04:27.110 --> 00:04:29.610
to the field of stereochemistry.

00:04:29.610 --> 00:04:33.800
With just this one discovery, Louis Pasteur
was able to fix a problem, and also pave the

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:37.240
way for future scientists in several fields
of chemistry.

00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:41.210
This alone would have been a huge accomplishment
that would have put his name down in history,

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:43.821
but he was far from finished with his life’s
work.

00:04:43.821 --> 00:04:52.770
Personal Life
Louis Pasteur met his future wife, Marie Laurent,

00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:55.259
while he was working at the University of
Strasbourg.

00:04:55.259 --> 00:04:59.490
Marie was the daughter of the school’s rector,
so she spent most of her life in an academic

00:04:59.490 --> 00:05:00.490
environment.

00:05:00.490 --> 00:05:03.909
She worked in the school’s laboratory, and
was assigned as Louis Pasteur’s lab assistant

00:05:03.909 --> 00:05:05.110
and “amanuensis.”

00:05:05.110 --> 00:05:09.110
This is someone who writes down the scientific
information, and assists in making sketches

00:05:09.110 --> 00:05:10.300
of his discoveries.

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:13.580
Marie was a very well-educated woman, and
she would have had a lot of scientific background

00:05:13.580 --> 00:05:16.539
knowledge in order for her to understand and
record the information.

00:05:16.539 --> 00:05:20.159
If she was born in a different time, there
is no doubt that she would have been a full-fledged

00:05:20.159 --> 00:05:21.180
chemist as well.

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:25.020
French society at the time found it to be
inappropriate for women to become scientists.

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:28.849
But Marie and Louis respected one another,
and the two of them were truly devoted to

00:05:28.849 --> 00:05:29.849
this work.

00:05:29.849 --> 00:05:34.080
It only makes sense that they were perfect
for one another both inside and outside of

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:35.080
the laboratory.

00:05:35.080 --> 00:05:38.520
Louis Pasteur was 25 years old when he made
the discoveries in wine sediments, and the

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:42.160
two of them were able to share the victory
of a job well done.

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:44.399
This would be only the beginning of their
teamwork.

00:05:44.399 --> 00:05:46.680
The two were married on May 29, 1849.

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:50.400
Louis Pasteur was 26, and Marie was 23.

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:53.909
As the years went on, Marie continued to assist
Louis in his experiments.

00:05:53.909 --> 00:05:57.219
Without her knowledge and professionalism
by his side, he may not have been able to

00:05:57.219 --> 00:05:59.690
accomplish as much as he did in his career.

00:05:59.690 --> 00:06:04.259
They went on to have five children together,
but tragically, only two would survive childhood.

00:06:04.259 --> 00:06:08.069
As parents who lost three children, they knew
the pain that all parents feel when their

00:06:08.069 --> 00:06:12.449
children die from a disease that could have
been prevented, if only there was a vaccine

00:06:12.449 --> 00:06:13.999
that existed to help them.

00:06:13.999 --> 00:06:17.839
The two of them became determined to devote
their careers to saving the lives of other

00:06:17.839 --> 00:07:21.860
children, and they were eventually able to
accomplish

00:07:21.860 --> 00:07:35.830
just that.

00:07:35.830 --> 00:08:14.649
Germ Theory and Pasteurization
Before he could begin studying diseases, Louis

00:08:14.649 --> 00:08:18.249
Pasteur still needed to do the scientific
research that was paying the bills.

00:08:18.249 --> 00:08:22.689
In 1854, Pasteur became a chemistry professor
at the University of Lille, and he was paid

00:08:22.689 --> 00:08:25.259
to continue researching issues in alcohol
manufacturing.

00:08:25.259 --> 00:08:29.550
He became the Dean of the Science department,
as well, and helped to mentor the rest of

00:08:29.550 --> 00:08:30.550
the faculty.

00:08:30.550 --> 00:08:35.419
In 1861, Pasteur discovered anaerobiosis,
which is crucial in understanding how yeast

00:08:35.419 --> 00:08:38.830
and sugar formed alcohol during the fermentation
process.

00:08:38.830 --> 00:08:42.340
Even though fermentation had been going on
for thousands of years of human history, no

00:08:42.340 --> 00:08:46.529
one else had been able to explain the “how”
and “why” of the chemical process.

00:08:46.529 --> 00:08:51.399
So, today, everyone running a hipster microbrewery
has Louis Pasteur to thank for their careers

00:08:51.399 --> 00:08:53.230
making craft beer.

00:08:53.230 --> 00:08:58.180
Once he fully understood the fermentation
process, Pasteur had a theory that these invisible

00:08:58.180 --> 00:09:03.250
organisms called “germs” and “bacteria”
were responsible for causing wine, beer, and

00:09:03.250 --> 00:09:04.440
milk to go sour.

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:08.351
He proved that by boiling these liquids, he
could kill the bacteria, and then cool to

00:09:08.351 --> 00:09:09.879
it to make it drinkable again.

00:09:09.879 --> 00:09:13.860
The first successful test was completed on
April 20th, 1862.

00:09:13.860 --> 00:09:19.310
That process of heating and cooling was named
after him; so it is now known as “pasteurization”.

00:09:19.310 --> 00:09:22.019
It is still used today.

00:09:22.019 --> 00:09:26.440
Louis Pasteur was able to move on from the
food industry, and begin studying disease,

00:09:26.440 --> 00:09:27.639
which had been his goal.

00:09:27.639 --> 00:09:32.060
In 1865, he was able to prove that microbes
were causing a disease that destroyed silkworm

00:09:32.060 --> 00:09:33.060
eggs.

00:09:33.060 --> 00:09:35.790
His wife, Marie, assisted him in growing the
silk worms, and keeping them alive during

00:09:35.790 --> 00:09:37.209
the course of the experiment.

00:09:37.209 --> 00:09:42.329
He was able to develop a prevention for contamination,
and saved the silk-making industry around

00:09:42.329 --> 00:09:43.329
the world.

00:09:43.329 --> 00:09:47.339
So, aside from our clean wine and beer, we
also need to thank him for our fancy silk

00:09:47.339 --> 00:09:48.889
robes and pajamas.

00:09:48.889 --> 00:09:52.690
Once he was beginning to understand how bacteria
caused illnesses, he was beginning to come

00:09:52.690 --> 00:09:54.129
up with his “germ theory”.

00:09:54.129 --> 00:09:58.050
For the majority of human history, people
did not know about the existence of germs,

00:09:58.050 --> 00:10:01.150
and they did not fully understand the immune
system.

00:10:01.150 --> 00:10:06.790
The widely-held belief was the “Miasma Theory”,
which was that diseases are caused by a poisonous

00:10:06.790 --> 00:10:10.889
vapor in the air that only came out at night.

00:10:10.889 --> 00:10:17.370
Yes, it sounds truly ridiculous in retrospect,
but people had absolutely no idea how or why

00:10:17.370 --> 00:10:19.399
human beings contracted illnesses.

00:10:19.399 --> 00:10:22.670
In hospitals, doctors and nurses never even
bothered to wash their hands, because they

00:10:22.670 --> 00:10:26.480
truly did not understand the spread of disease.

00:10:26.480 --> 00:10:29.750
Human waste would sometimes make its way into
the water system, which would trigger huge

00:10:29.750 --> 00:10:34.579
outbreaks of infectious disease, all because
people did not understand that germs existed.

00:10:34.579 --> 00:10:39.500
Other scientists had proposed a germ theory
long before Louis Pasteur, but it was often

00:10:39.500 --> 00:10:40.500
dismissed.

00:10:40.500 --> 00:10:45.170
People had believed in Miasma Theory for so
long, it had become the truth.

00:10:45.170 --> 00:10:48.639
In England, a scientist named Dr. John Snow
(Not to be confused with the character from

00:10:48.639 --> 00:10:54.180
Game of Thrones) was able to save London from
the cholera outbreak by convincing the town

00:10:54.180 --> 00:10:57.189
to shut down a water pump that had been infected
by human waste.

00:10:57.189 --> 00:11:00.960
Louis Pasteur would have probably read about
Snow’s breakthrough in bacterial research,

00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:04.000
and it would inspire him to push the germ
theory as well.

00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:08.150
Soon enough, Pasteur and the other fathers
of microbiology were able to prove that the

00:11:08.150 --> 00:11:14.029
Miasma Theory was nothing but a fantasy, and
the acknowledgement that germs existed would

00:11:14.029 --> 00:11:17.529
change the medical industry was we know it.

00:11:17.529 --> 00:11:25.860
The Accidental Invention of Vaccines
In 1868, Louis Pasteur was just 46 years old

00:11:25.860 --> 00:11:29.970
when he suffered a severe stroke, and he became
partially paralyzed.

00:11:29.970 --> 00:11:33.329
Instead of going to a hospital, Pasteur was
treated in his home.

00:11:33.329 --> 00:11:36.949
Sixteen leeches were placed behind his ears
to clear the blood that had pooled around

00:11:36.949 --> 00:11:38.620
his cerebral hemorrhage.

00:11:38.620 --> 00:11:43.060
After the doctor left with the leeches, Louis
Pasteur was left in his wife Marie.

00:11:43.060 --> 00:11:46.850
He was very lucky that she had such an extensive
science background, because she helped to

00:11:46.850 --> 00:11:49.940
rehabilitate him to overcome his paralysis.

00:11:49.940 --> 00:11:53.600
This story is usually glossed over in many
of the history books.

00:11:53.600 --> 00:11:58.420
We tend to glorify the major accomplishments
of successful people, and ignore the struggles.

00:11:58.420 --> 00:12:03.290
Louis Pasteur was still very human, and recovering
from a stroke is never an easy task.

00:12:03.290 --> 00:12:07.459
Back in the 1800’s, a stroke would likely
mean death, or permanent paralysis for the

00:12:07.459 --> 00:12:08.459
rest of his life.

00:12:08.459 --> 00:12:12.339
Marie nursed him back to health, and encouraged
him to heal so that they could both return

00:12:12.339 --> 00:12:16.589
to the laboratory in order to continue their
life’s work of curing diseases.

00:12:16.589 --> 00:12:19.870
Even though his health recovered, it would
be years before Louis Pasteur was able to

00:12:19.870 --> 00:12:22.480
make another major scientific discovery.

00:12:22.480 --> 00:12:26.980
Eight years later, in 1876, Louis Pasteur
made his first major breakthrough since having

00:12:26.980 --> 00:12:27.980
his stroke.

00:12:27.980 --> 00:12:32.430
At the time, scientists believed in the idea
of “spontaneous generation”.

00:12:32.430 --> 00:12:36.749
This was the ridiculous notion that living
creatures could spontaneously generate from

00:12:36.749 --> 00:12:38.100
inanimate substances.

00:12:38.100 --> 00:12:42.129
People believed that living creatures were
“born” asexually, like mice forming in

00:12:42.129 --> 00:12:45.329
rotting food, food and frogs emerging from
the mud.

00:12:45.329 --> 00:12:48.890
This belief was so common, there were even
some scientific papers claiming to come up

00:12:48.890 --> 00:12:51.569
with a “recipe” for growing your own mice.

00:12:51.569 --> 00:12:55.620
Pasteur publicly denounced this age-old belief
of spontaneous generation, but there were

00:12:55.620 --> 00:12:57.839
still plenty of people who had their doubts.

00:12:57.839 --> 00:13:02.439
In the past, other scientists like Francesco
Redi had attempted to prove that spontaneous

00:13:02.439 --> 00:13:03.960
generation was not real.

00:13:03.960 --> 00:13:07.839
He sealed a piece of meat in a jar with a
lid, proving that as long as flies did not

00:13:07.839 --> 00:13:10.269
land on food, larva did not appear.

00:13:10.269 --> 00:13:14.290
However, people did not believe this, because
they thought oxygen may be necessary for the

00:13:14.290 --> 00:13:15.290
so-called “recipe”.

00:13:15.290 --> 00:13:20.279
The only way Pasteur was able to convince
the public that animals are not born out of

00:13:20.279 --> 00:13:25.579
food was to design custom swan-necked bottles
that curved in such a way that allowed oxygen

00:13:25.579 --> 00:13:29.310
to get through, but it would be impossible
for flies to crawl inside.

00:13:29.310 --> 00:13:33.490
He boiled beef broth, and left it inside this
swan neck bottle.

00:13:33.490 --> 00:13:36.820
Finally, the idea of spontaneous generation
was debunked.

00:13:36.820 --> 00:13:42.160
Two years later, in 1878, Pasteur was studying
diseases when he accidently exposed chickens

00:13:42.160 --> 00:13:44.779
to an attenuated culture of chicken cholera.

00:13:44.779 --> 00:13:47.360
However, the chickens did not die from the
disease.

00:13:47.360 --> 00:13:50.120
In fact, it had the exact opposite.

00:13:50.120 --> 00:13:54.529
When they were later exposed to the full-fledged
chicken cholera virus, they had become immune.

00:13:54.529 --> 00:13:57.660
This was his very first vaccine.

00:13:57.660 --> 00:14:01.910
At the time, the idea that he could replicate
these results in human beings was still years

00:14:01.910 --> 00:14:03.220
away from actually happening.

00:14:03.220 --> 00:14:07.790
But he theorized that if he could replicate
that same process, he just may be able to

00:14:07.790 --> 00:14:13.300
create vaccines for some of the deadliest
diseases of the time, like anthrax, tuberculosis,

00:14:13.300 --> 00:14:15.029
smallpox, and anthrax.

00:14:15.029 --> 00:14:19.839
A year later, in 1881, Louis Pasteur developed
a vaccine for Anthrax, and cured several sheep

00:14:19.839 --> 00:14:21.820
in his laboratory from the disease.

00:14:21.820 --> 00:14:25.600
Even after conducting the same experiment
hundreds of times, the scientific community

00:14:25.600 --> 00:14:28.810
still did not believe Louis Pasteur’s results.

00:14:28.810 --> 00:14:33.189
Even when everyone mocked and ridiculed him,
he did not back down, because he knew that

00:14:33.189 --> 00:14:34.189
he was right.

00:14:34.189 --> 00:14:37.550
For years, people refused to believe him,
until he traveled to the town of Pouilly le

00:14:37.550 --> 00:14:41.120
Fort, which had a farm filled with sheep that
had been infected with Anthrax.

00:14:41.120 --> 00:14:45.339
Pasteur injected his vaccine into all of the
sick sheep in front of a large crowd of people.

00:14:45.339 --> 00:14:49.550
Sure enough, all of the sheep recovered, and
the newspapers finally declared that he truly

00:14:49.550 --> 00:14:50.910
had invented the cure.

00:14:50.910 --> 00:14:55.819
He once wrote this advice to future generations
on how to deal with haters; “Do not let

00:14:55.819 --> 00:14:59.350
yourself be tainted with a barren skepticism.”

00:14:59.350 --> 00:15:03.310
After Louis Pasteur’s germ theory was published
in academic papers, and there was a significant

00:15:03.310 --> 00:15:06.310
amount of proof that his animal vaccines were
working.

00:15:06.310 --> 00:15:10.509
Educated people with open minds understood
the concept of bacteria, and they began to

00:15:10.509 --> 00:15:14.319
make efforts to improve public health and
safety in France.

00:15:14.319 --> 00:15:18.390
Following Louis Pasteur’s discoveries about
germs, a politician named Eugene Poubelle

00:15:18.390 --> 00:15:21.550
began enforcing the first garbage cans in
Paris in 1884.

00:15:21.550 --> 00:15:25.269
At first, citizens were outraged that they
could no longer throw their trash wherever

00:15:25.269 --> 00:15:26.330
they wanted.

00:15:26.330 --> 00:15:30.339
Despite dealing with all of the skepticism
and push-back from the public, Poubelle continued

00:15:30.339 --> 00:15:33.760
to mandate that garbage cans were necessary
in every home.

00:15:33.760 --> 00:15:38.649
Today, we take garbage cans for granted, but
this was a huge step in disease prevention.

00:15:38.649 --> 00:15:42.519
Over the next few years, the spread of disease
began to fall dramatically in France, and

00:15:42.519 --> 00:15:45.059
other countries around the world began to
copy the idea.

00:15:45.059 --> 00:15:50.740
However, just like every other scientific
discovery in human history, there were people

00:15:50.740 --> 00:15:55.389
who still refused to believe in the germ theory,
despite all of the proof that was out there.

00:15:55.389 --> 00:15:59.129
Pasteur had plenty of critics who still tried
to claim that he was incorrect.

00:15:59.129 --> 00:16:04.339
Louis Pasteur had a bitter rivalry with another
scientist named Antoine Béchamp, who completely

00:16:04.339 --> 00:16:06.100
dismissed his germ theory.

00:16:06.100 --> 00:16:10.769
Instead of believing in the existence of germs,
Béchamp published his own theory of “pleomorphism”.

00:16:10.769 --> 00:16:15.220
But, of course, the truth always has a way
of coming out, and Pasteur continued to be

00:16:15.220 --> 00:16:19.769
the one who came out on top.

00:16:19.769 --> 00:16:25.110
The Rabies Vaccine
Louis Pasteur continued to prove to the world

00:16:25.110 --> 00:16:29.059
that his germ theory was correct, and that
infections were the cause of disease.

00:16:29.059 --> 00:16:32.500
At the time, rabies and anthrax were both
very serious problems in animals.

00:16:32.500 --> 00:16:37.800
If a dog was infected and bit someone, it
was guaranteed that they would die an excruciatingly

00:16:37.800 --> 00:16:38.899
painful death.

00:16:38.899 --> 00:16:42.449
After years of overcoming so much doubt in
the scientific community, Louis Pasteur was

00:16:42.449 --> 00:16:46.290
now well regarded for his achievements in
science and medicine.

00:16:46.290 --> 00:16:50.709
In 1873, The Académie de Médecine elected
to bring him on as an associate member of

00:16:50.709 --> 00:16:51.709
their society.

00:16:51.709 --> 00:16:54.310
And in 1882, he was accepted into the Académie
Française.

00:16:54.310 --> 00:16:59.769
While he was doing his research there, he
decided to devote his research to curing rabies.

00:16:59.769 --> 00:17:04.329
In 1883, Pasteur was creating the rabies vaccine
by acquiring rabbits that had been infected

00:17:04.329 --> 00:17:08.090
by the virus, and attempting to inject the
vaccine into dogs.

00:17:08.090 --> 00:17:11.589
After trial and error, he was able to cure
dogs who had been infected, and bring them

00:17:11.589 --> 00:17:12.589
back to health.

00:17:12.589 --> 00:17:16.900
But using the vaccine on a human being had
not been done until 1885.

00:17:16.900 --> 00:17:21.350
On July 6th, a 9-year old boy named Joseph
Meister was brought to Louis Pasteur in Paris.

00:17:21.350 --> 00:17:25.760
His parents had traveled for two days from
Alsace after he was bitten by a rabid dog.

00:17:25.760 --> 00:17:30.020
At first, Pasteur hesitated, because he had
only ever experimented with the vaccine on

00:17:30.020 --> 00:17:31.020
dogs.

00:17:31.020 --> 00:17:33.880
But the boy was going to die anyway, so Pasteur
agreed to try.

00:17:33.880 --> 00:17:38.159
He took an emulsion from the spinal cord of
a rabbit who had rabies, and formed a vaccine.

00:17:38.159 --> 00:17:39.720
The process did not happen overnight.

00:17:39.720 --> 00:17:43.370
Over the next 10 days, Joseph was given 13
more injections of the vaccine, and he was

00:17:43.370 --> 00:17:45.409
hospitalized for three months.

00:17:45.409 --> 00:17:49.640
After the boy made a full recovery, Louis
Pasteur became famous around the world for

00:17:49.640 --> 00:17:50.799
finding the cure.

00:17:50.799 --> 00:17:54.600
On the 20th of October that same year, an
adult male who had been infected with rabies

00:17:54.600 --> 00:17:57.059
six days earlier was brought to Louis Pasteur.

00:17:57.059 --> 00:18:00.559
He repeated the same vaccination process as
before, and the man made a full recovery.

00:18:00.559 --> 00:18:03.630
Now, there was no doubt that the vaccine truly
worked.

00:18:03.630 --> 00:18:08.440
In 1886, Louis Pasteur began to treat over
350 patients who had been infected with rabies

00:18:08.440 --> 00:18:10.210
in Europe and the United States.

00:18:10.210 --> 00:18:13.830
Louis Pasteur’s wife, Marie, traveled the
world with him, and nursed the sick children

00:18:13.830 --> 00:18:18.630
with rabies for months at a time while her
husband was preparing the vaccines and giving

00:18:18.630 --> 00:18:19.690
the injections.

00:18:19.690 --> 00:18:24.480
Of course, today, this same vaccine is what
helps keep our dogs and cats safe from rabies.

00:18:24.480 --> 00:18:29.340
No matter where he went, whether he could
speak the language or not, the story was always

00:18:29.340 --> 00:18:30.340
the same.

00:18:30.340 --> 00:18:34.169
He was very proud that he was able to accomplish
this on behalf of France.

00:18:34.169 --> 00:18:38.480
He said, “Science knows no country, because
knowledge belongs to humanity, and is the

00:18:38.480 --> 00:18:40.919
torch which illuminates the world.

00:18:40.919 --> 00:18:44.890
Science is the highest personification of
the nation, because that nation will remain

00:18:44.890 --> 00:18:49.340
the first which carries the furthest the works
of thought and intelligence.”

00:18:49.340 --> 00:18:53.390
During this process of meeting and healing
over 350 people, Pasteur got to know many

00:18:53.390 --> 00:18:54.419
of his patients.

00:18:54.419 --> 00:18:57.480
Many of them were young children who were
bitten when playing with their dogs.

00:18:57.480 --> 00:19:02.870
He said, “When I approach a child, he inspires
in me two sentiments; tenderness for what

00:19:02.870 --> 00:19:06.100
he is, and respect for what he may become.”

00:19:06.100 --> 00:19:10.080
Louis Pasteur wanted to save the lives of
these sick children so that they could have

00:19:10.080 --> 00:19:14.890
a chance to grow up, and he wanted his vaccines
to do the same for future generations all

00:19:14.890 --> 00:19:15.940
over the world.

00:19:15.940 --> 00:19:20.050
Finally, he was recognized as a scientist
worthy of his own research facility, and a

00:19:20.050 --> 00:19:24.240
fundraising campaign began to create the Pasteur
Institute in Paris.

00:19:24.240 --> 00:19:27.559
This was finally inaugurated on November 14,
1888.

00:19:27.559 --> 00:19:31.550
When he turned 70 years old, he was given
a spectacular birthday party at The University

00:19:31.550 --> 00:19:36.899
of Paris, and some of the most prestigious
doctors and scientists were there to celebrate

00:19:36.899 --> 00:19:37.909
his life and works.

00:19:37.909 --> 00:19:42.110
Louis Pasteur he died in 1895, at the age
of 73.

00:19:42.110 --> 00:19:45.390
His body is now in a crypt in the Pasteur
Institute.

00:19:45.390 --> 00:19:48.390
But his work has lived on far after his passing.

00:19:48.390 --> 00:19:52.830
Today, we still use pasteurization in the
food industry, and his breakthroughs in medicine

00:19:52.830 --> 00:19:54.760
were just the beginning.

00:19:54.760 --> 00:19:58.970
Other scientists began to use the same methods
to create vaccines for diseases that had once

00:19:58.970 --> 00:20:00.500
killed thousands of people.

00:20:00.500 --> 00:20:04.539
There is no telling just how many millions
of people would have died without the work

00:20:04.539 --> 00:20:37.130
he was able 
to accomplish.

